{"archetypes":[{"name":"schema-wire-consistency","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The 18-column TSV schema is stated verbatim in at least four locations (plan prose, tally script header comment, harness fixture assertion, docs/run-logs.md paragraph) and the parser's five KV output keys must map unambiguously onto the three vN column groups — cross-location drift is a common plan defect for multi-file schema changes.","prompt_body":"Audit every location in the plan and in the referenced files where the 18-column TSV schema is named, quoted, or implied: the column-header string in tally-plan-review.sh, the harness fixture assertion in test-findings-classification.sh, the docs/run-logs.md paragraph, and the emit_kv output keys of parse-judge-vote-and-rating.sh (PARSED_VOTE, PARSED_CORRECTNESS, PARSED_SEVERITY, PARSED_QUALITY, PARSED_UNCERTAIN). Verify that the exact column names (case, underscore placement, order) are consistent across all locations and that the tally's row-assembly step has an unambiguous, deterministic way to derive the five-field vN group per slot from the parser's five KV lines. Check whether the plan specifies how tally derives the tool-name-to-vN mapping when a voter filename does not match the expected claude/codex/cursor pattern, since the plan simultaneously says to raise an error on unrecognized filenames but the harness does not appear to include a fixture for that path. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"parser-exit-contract","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The parser's exit-0 guarantee is load-bearing for set -euo pipefail callers in tally-plan-review.sh and the future tally-code-votes.sh, but the plan has a gap: it specifies exit 0 for 'no ID line present' but does not clearly resolve exit behavior when the ID IS present but the vote token is unrecognized — the two callers may behave differently depending on how this case is handled.","prompt_body":"Read parse-judge-vote-and-rating.sh and parse-judge-vote-and-rating.md if they exist, then compare the plan's parser-contract section against the actual implementation. Identify the exact exit-code and PARSED_VOTE value for each input case: (a) unreadable file or missing args, (b) voter file exists but contains no line matching the ballot ID, (c) ID-matching line with a recognized vote token, (d) ID-matching line with an unrecognized vote token — the plan implies case (d) should produce exit 0 with empty PARSED_VOTE but does not state this explicitly, and a non-zero exit would abort tally under set -euo pipefail. Also verify that the UNCERTAIN defaulting rule ('true when any axis was missing or unrecognized') is stated consistently in the parser contract, the harness cases, and the tally row-assembly logic — specifically check whether a judge line that has UNCERTAIN=false but omits one other axis actually produces PARSED_UNCERTAIN=true as the plan requires, and whether the harness includes a fixture for exactly this case. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"publish-allowlist-safety","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The design-log-publish.sh allowlist extension introduces a find-glob for plan-review/round-*/findings-classification.tsv with a reject-on-unexpected clause, but the plan's description of this block is sparse relative to the existing render-cache/ pattern it claims to mirror — insufficient specificity here risks either silent TSV drops or unredacted artifact leakage.","prompt_body":"Read the current design-log-publish.sh to understand how the existing render-cache/ staging block is structured: how it enumerates files, how it invokes design_publish_stage_file, and how it rejects unexpected files with larch_err. Evaluate whether the plan's description of the new plan-review/ staging block is specific enough to produce a correct implementation: (1) does a find command matching plan-review/round-*/findings-classification.tsv under DESIGN_TMPDIR produce relative paths that align with the RUN_DEST subdirectory calculation used elsewhere in the publisher, (2) does the 'reject unexpected files' clause define what counts as unexpected precisely enough to prevent a future findings-classification-v2.tsv or round-N/debug.txt from slipping through, and (3) does the plan handle the case where plan-review/ exists but contains no round-N subdirectories without emitting a spurious larch_err. Check whether test-design-log-publish.sh currently exists and whether the plan's testing section is consistent with the actual harness coverage described there. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
